  ["PostgreSQL", "A query is slow. How do you troubleshoot it?", "troubleshoot-slow-query", "Explain the diagnostic steps.", "**Direct answer :**\n1. Run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the query. 2. Look for sequential scans, sorts, high cost. 3. Check if indexes are used. 4. Verify statistics (`ANALYZE`). 5. Check for locks (`pg_locks`, `pg_stat_activity`). 6. Consider query rewriting or adding indexes. 7. Check resource utilization (CPU, I/O, memory).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the diagnostic steps beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Run `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the query. 2. Look for sequential scans, sorts, high cost. 3. Check if indexes are used. 4. Verify statistics (`ANALYZE`). 5. Check for locks (`pg_locks`, `pg_stat_activity`). 6. Consider query rewriting or adding indexes. 7. Check resource utilization (CPU, I/O, memory). The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you handle table bloat?", "table-bloat", "Explain the problem and solution.", "**Direct answer :**\nBloat occurs from dead rows caused by updates/deletes without VACUUM. Use `VACUUM` regularly. For severe bloat, use `VACUUM FULL` (locks table) or `pg_repack` (online). Monitor bloat with `pgstattuple` extension.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the problem and solution beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nBloat occurs from dead rows caused by updates/deletes without VACUUM. Use `VACUUM` regularly. For severe bloat, use `VACUUM FULL` (locks table) or `pg_repack` (online). Monitor bloat with `pgstattuple` extension. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "A database is out of disk space. What do you do?", "disk-space-full", "Explain the recovery steps.", "**Direct answer :**\n1. Check free space: `df -h`. 2. Identify large objects: `SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('table'))`. 3. Remove old logs, WAL files if safe. 4. Consider vacuuming to free dead space. 5. Add more storage. 6. Archive old data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the recovery steps beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Check free space: `df -h`. 2. Identify large objects: `SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('table'))`. 3. Remove old logs, WAL files if safe. 4. Consider vacuuming to free dead space. 5. Add more storage. 6. Archive old data. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to connect the concept to a real PostgreSQL workload and explain the main operational or performance trade-off."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you restore a table from a dump without the entire database?", "restore-single-table", "Explain the procedure.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse `pg_restore` with `-t` (table) or `--table` from a custom dump. For plain SQL, you can extract the table section manually. You can also use `pg_dump -t table_name` to backup only that table.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the procedure beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse `pg_restore` with `-t` (table) or `--table` from a custom dump. For plain SQL, you can extract the table section manually. You can also use `pg_dump -t table_name` to backup only that table. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you delete a very large table without causing performance issues?", "delete-large-table", "Explain the best practices.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse `TRUNCATE` instead of `DELETE` for deleting all rows (fast but not DML). For selective deletion, break into batches with `DELETE ... WHERE ... LIMIT 1000` inside a loop. Use `VACUUM` afterward.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the best practices beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse `TRUNCATE` instead of `DELETE` for deleting all rows (fast but not DML). For selective deletion, break into batches with `DELETE ... WHERE ... LIMIT 1000` inside a loop. Use `VACUUM` afterward. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you replicate data to another PostgreSQL instance?", "replication-setup", "Explain the methods.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse physical streaming replication (built-in) or logical replication (table-level). For physical, configure `wal_level = replica`, set up `recovery.conf` on standby. For logical, create publications and subscriptions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the methods beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse physical streaming replication (built-in) or logical replication (table-level). For physical, configure `wal_level = replica`, set up `recovery.conf` on standby. For logical, create publications and subscriptions. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you handle a corrupted database?", "db-corruption", "Explain recovery options.", "**Direct answer :**\nIf the database is corrupted, you can restore from a known good backup. If a specific table is corrupt, use `pg_dump` to dump that table (might work). If the WAL is intact, use `pg_resetwal` (risky). Otherwise, use tools like `pg_repack` or `pg_verifybackup`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain recovery options beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nIf the database is corrupted, you can restore from a known good backup. If a specific table is corrupt, use `pg_dump` to dump that table (might work). If the WAL is intact, use `pg_resetwal` (risky). Otherwise, use tools like `pg_repack` or `pg_verifybackup`. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you manage user roles and permissions in a multi-tenant application?", "multi-tenant-roles", "Explain the strategy.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse separate schemas per tenant, with a role per tenant. Grant permissions to those roles on their own schema. Use row-level security (RLS) if tenants share a schema. Use `SET ROLE` to switch context.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the strategy beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse separate schemas per tenant, with a role per tenant. Grant permissions to those roles on their own schema. Use row-level security (RLS) if tenants share a schema. Use `SET ROLE` to switch context. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the difference between `pg_dump` and `pg_dumpall`?", "pg-dump-vs-dumpall", "Compare the two.", "**Direct answer :**\n`pg_dump` backs up a single database. `pg_dumpall` backs up all databases and global objects (roles, tablespaces). Use `pg_dumpall` for full cluster backup.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Compare the two beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`pg_dump` backs up a single database. `pg_dumpall` backs up all databases and global objects (roles, tablespaces). Use `pg_dumpall` for full cluster backup. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain the practical trade-off, when you would choose each option, and one example where the distinction affects correctness or performance."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you upgrade PostgreSQL to a new major version?", "pg-upgrade", "Explain the process.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse `pg_upgrade` for in-place upgrades (with `--link` for fast migration). Also, use `pg_dumpall` + `psql` for logical upgrades. Always test and have backups. For logical upgrades, use `pg_dump` and restore to the new version.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the process beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse `pg_upgrade` for in-place upgrades (with `--link` for fast migration). Also, use `pg_dumpall` + `psql` for logical upgrades. Always test and have backups. For logical upgrades, use `pg_dump` and restore to the new version. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the `pg_stat_bgwriter` view used for?", "pg-stat-bgwriter", "Explain its purpose.", "**Direct answer :**\n`pg_stat_bgwriter` shows statistics about the background writer process, which writes dirty buffers to disk. It helps tune checkpoint and write performance. Metrics include buffers_clean, buffers_checkpoint, maxwritten_clean.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain its purpose beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`pg_stat_bgwriter` shows statistics about the background writer process, which writes dirty buffers to disk. It helps tune checkpoint and write performance. Metrics include buffers_clean, buffers_checkpoint, maxwritten_clean. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain when you would use this in a real PostgreSQL system, what trade-off it introduces, and how you would diagnose problems involving it."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you handle out-of-memory (OOM) issues?", "oom-issues", "Explain the strategies.", "**Direct answer :**\nIncrease shared memory (`shared_buffers`, `work_mem`). Reduce `max_connections`. Use connection pooling (PgBouncer). Adjust Linux kernel parameters (`vm.overcommit_memory`). Monitor memory usage with `pg_stat_activity`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the strategies beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nIncrease shared memory (`shared_buffers`, `work_mem`). Reduce `max_connections`. Use connection pooling (PgBouncer). Adjust Linux kernel parameters (`vm.overcommit_memory`). Monitor memory usage with `pg_stat_activity`. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the purpose of `pg_restore` options `-j` and `-F`?", "pg-restore-options", "Explain the options.", "**Direct answer :**\n`-j` specifies the number of parallel workers for restore (improves performance). `-F` specifies the format (`c` for custom, `d` for directory format). Use both for faster large restores.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the options beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`-j` specifies the number of parallel workers for restore (improves performance). `-F` specifies the format (`c` for custom, `d` for directory format). Use both for faster large restores. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain when you would use this in a real PostgreSQL system, what trade-off it introduces, and how you would diagnose problems involving it."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you tune PostgreSQL for high write throughput?", "tune-for-write", "Explain configuration changes.", "**Direct answer :**\nIncrease `checkpoint_completion_target` to spread writes. Increase `max_wal_size` to reduce checkpoint frequency. Use `synchronous_commit = off` (if acceptable). Use RAID 10 for WAL. Adjust `commit_delay` and `commit_siblings`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain configuration changes beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nIncrease `checkpoint_completion_target` to spread writes. Increase `max_wal_size` to reduce checkpoint frequency. Use `synchronous_commit = off` (if acceptable). Use RAID 10 for WAL. Adjust `commit_delay` and `commit_siblings`. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you handle connection saturation?", "connection-saturation", "Explain solutions.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse a connection pooler like PgBouncer. Tune `max_connections`. Optimize queries to release connections quickly. Monitor active connections and idle-in-transaction connections.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain solutions beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse a connection pooler like PgBouncer. Tune `max_connections`. Optimize queries to release connections quickly. Monitor active connections and idle-in-transaction connections. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the difference between `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` and `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW`?", "refresh-mv-concurrently", "Explain the difference.", "**Direct answer :**\n`REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` locks the table and blocks reads. `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` refreshes without locking, using a temporary table; it requires a unique index on the view. Use concurrently for high-availability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the difference beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` locks the table and blocks reads. `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` refreshes without locking, using a temporary table; it requires a unique index on the view. Use concurrently for high-availability. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain the practical trade-off, when you would choose each option, and one example where the distinction affects correctness or performance."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you perform a rolling upgrade of PostgreSQL?", "rolling-upgrade", "Explain the strategy.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse logical replication to replicate changes to a new version server. Gradually switch application traffic. Or use pg_upgrade with minimal downtime (using `--link`). Plan for failover and rollback.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the strategy beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nUse logical replication to replicate changes to a new version server. Gradually switch application traffic. Or use pg_upgrade with minimal downtime (using `--link`). Plan for failover and rollback. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the purpose of `pg_stat_replication`?", "pg-stat-replication", "Explain the view.", "**Direct answer :**\n`pg_stat_replication` shows replication status: standby names, WAL send location, write lag, flush lag, replay lag. It helps monitor replication health and latency.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the view beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`pg_stat_replication` shows replication status: standby names, WAL send location, write lag, flush lag, replay lag. It helps monitor replication health and latency. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain when you would use this in a real PostgreSQL system, what trade-off it introduces, and how you would diagnose problems involving it."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "How do you handle time zones in PostgreSQL?", "timezone-handling", "Explain the approach.", "**Direct answer :**\nFor an instant in time, PostgreSQL's `timestamp with time zone` (`timestamptz`) is usually the right type. PostgreSQL normalizes the value for storage and displays it according to the session time zone. Use `AT TIME ZONE` when you need an explicit conversion between an instant and a local wall-clock timestamp. The important design decision is to distinguish instants from values that intentionally represent a local date/time without a time zone.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the approach beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\nFor an instant in time, PostgreSQL's `timestamp with time zone` (`timestamptz`) is usually the right type. PostgreSQL normalizes the value for storage and displays it according to the session time zone. Use `AT TIME ZONE` when you need an explicit conversion between an instant and a local wall-clock timestamp. The important design decision is to distinguish instants from values that intentionally represent a local date/time without a time zone. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to describe the exact steps, the checks you would perform before running them in production, and how you would verify the result."],
  ["PostgreSQL", "What is the `pg_notify` function used for?", "pg-notify", "Explain the asynchronous notification.", "**Direct answer :**\n`pg_notify` sends a notification to a channel. Used with `LISTEN` in clients to receive asynchronous events (e.g., for cache invalidation). It is part of the LISTEN/NOTIFY feature.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand Explain the asynchronous notification beyond memorized terminology. They want to see that you can explain its purpose, choose it appropriately, and recognize the effect it has on correctness, performance, concurrency, or operations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n`pg_notify` sends a notification to a channel. Used with `LISTEN` in clients to receive asynchronous events (e.g., for cache invalidation). It is part of the LISTEN/NOTIFY feature. The important engineering point is to understand not only what PostgreSQL does, but why the behavior matters in a real workload. Consider the data size, query pattern, transaction behavior, locking, resource usage, and operational risk before applying the technique. Validate the decision with appropriate PostgreSQL metadata, execution plans, monitoring, or a controlled test rather than assuming a setting or command is always beneficial.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the definition or purpose in one sentence. Then explain the key mechanism or syntax, followed by the main benefit and trade-off. Finish with a short production example showing when you would use it and what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nBe ready to explain when you would use this in a real PostgreSQL system, what trade-off it introduces, and how you would diagnose problems involving it."]
